diction in the country, and the open presidential race there is not considered competitive. The incumbent Republican president is term-limited, removing any incumbency effect, and no public polls, expert ratings, or prediction markets treat the Republican ticket as viable in the district. Democrats have carried DC by overwhelming margins in every recent presidential election, and recent headlines about national fights over the district's governance and mail-in voting show no sign of changing that pattern.\n\nThe Democratic ticket is positioned to win by a margin in the neighborhood of fifty points, with the Republican share limited to a small, largely symbolic vote. Fundraising and outside spending are not meaningful factors here, and the main uncertainty is only how large the Democratic margin will be, not who wins.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9995, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 1.0, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 1.0, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9999, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 1.0, "margin": 50.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9999, "margin": 52.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9989, "margin": 44.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9998, "margin": 50.0}]}